#!/usr/bin/env bash
# pre-commit -- runs check_citations.py against the tree that will actually
# be committed, not against whatever happens to be sitting on disk.
#
# dipankarsarkar, round 5: "Is 'cited' something you want computed or
# curated? A short pre-commit check ... would have returned 6 this morning
# instead of 0." This is that check wired into git, not left as a script
# someone has to remember to run by hand -- which is the exact asymmetry
# he named (the TAG layer got a mechanism, the citation layer got a
# one-time manual sweep).
#
# Round 6, same reviewer, demonstrated directly: `git merge --no-ff`
# doesn't fire pre-commit at all -- only pre-merge-commit does, and that
# hook didn't exist. A defect committed on a side branch with --no-verify
# landed on main clean, no hook output, nothing caught until someone
# manually reran the checker. install-hooks.sh now installs this same
# script under both hook names.
#
# Round 7, same reviewer, again: classify() reads REPO_ROOT off disk --
# os.path.isfile, glob.glob, os.path.exists(".sha256"), the hash reread in
# seal_is_current(). None of that is git. So a file `git add`-ed alongside
# a citation to it, while its own newly-created dependency sits untracked
# on disk, checks green here and fails on any clone -- the exploit was
# demonstrated, not hypothetical (a doc + its seal staged, the script it
# cites left untracked; disk-based check: exit 0; the actual committed
# tree: ABSENT, exit 1). His answer to "should a seal be a claim about the
# disk or the commit": the commit -- HEAD is the only object a stranger
# can ever see, the disk never leaves this machine. `git write-tree` is
# the index as it stands right now, which is exactly what `git commit`
# is about to turn into a commit -- including partial `git add -p` staging,
# which raw disk state doesn't respect either. Extract that tree to a
# scratch dir and run the checker against the extraction, not REPO_ROOT.
#
# Round 8, same reviewer: the whole citation layer above only covers
# EXP-*.md / FINDING__*.md / README*.md. A .jsonl dataset never matches
# DOC_NAME_RE, so it's never scanned at all -- and even if it were,
# looks_like_path() explicitly returns False for anything starting with
# http:// or https://, so a citation URL is invisible on top of that.
# scripts/check_dataset_citations.py is the missing bucket: scans every
# *.jsonl under AI_EXPERIMENTS/DATASETS*/, checks that each "citation"
# field's URL(s) actually resolve. It does NOT check that a record's
# content matches what the URL says -- that layer (three Apollo-sourced
# records misstated which models a table covered, all found by hand
# against the actual paper) has no automated mechanism and isn't claimed
# to; source_locator fields naming the exact table/section are the manual
# discipline for that, not something this script enforces.
#
# Round 19, same reviewer: "What is supposed to check a seal that nothing
# cites?" Both checks above are citation-driven -- they only ever look at
# a file because some doc mentioned its name. scripts/citation_baseline.txt,
# scripts/dataset_citation_baseline.txt, and scripts/install-hooks.sh are
# never cited by any doc, so all three sat stale for weeks (one of them
# broken again by this very hook's own maintenance a commit ago) with
# every check above exiting 0. scripts/check_seals.py is citation-
# independent: it walks every *.sha256 in the tree and verifies it against
# its target's current content regardless of whether anything ever names
# that file.
#
# Round 46, same reviewer: proposed a one-line standing check --
# all(json.dumps(json.loads(l), ensure_ascii=False) == l for l in lines) --
# for the exact regression class round 43 introduced and round 44 fixed by
# hand (a re-serialization that silently \u-escapes real Cyrillic text,
# changing zero parsed values so every content-level invariant here stays
# green while the raw-line ctrl-F contract this project has repeatedly
# named breaks). Reproduced independently: 0/6/0 fails across
# e67a6ee~1/e67a6ee/72b0020, exact same 6 lines named. Confirmed the naive
# alternative ("no \u in the raw line") is wrong on a genuinely correct
# file -- misbehavior_raw_candidates_ui_archives_v1.jsonl has lines whose
# quote field is a verbatim archived ChatGPT tool-call argument that was
# already \u-escaped at capture time; the round-trip check passes them
# (ensure_ascii=False never touches literal ASCII text, wherever it came
# from), a grep would not. scripts/check_jsonl_canonical.py is that check,
# wired in here. Found live, on first run against every tracked *.jsonl:
# one more instance of the same regression, in a dataset this hook's
# citation-driven checks never had reason to open
# (DATASETS_VULN_6GROUPS_EXTERNAL/06_stop_gate_pressure_external.jsonl,
# line 4, an escaped em-dash) -- fixed the same way round 44 fixed the
# first six.
#
# .git/hooks/ is not tracked by git, so this file lives in scripts/ and has
# to be installed once per clone: scripts/install-hooks.sh does that.
set -euo pipefail
cd "$(git rev-parse --show-toplevel)"
tmp="$(mktemp -d)"
trap 'rm -rf "$tmp"' EXIT
git archive --format=tar "$(git write-tree)" | tar -x -C "$tmp"
python3 "$tmp/scripts/check_citations.py"
python3 "$tmp/scripts/check_dataset_citations.py"
python3 "$tmp/scripts/check_seals.py" "$tmp"
python3 "$tmp/scripts/check_jsonl_canonical.py" "$tmp"
